footnote.body></footnote></name></outline.name.block></front><chapter.body date.updated="0"><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">I</designator><name date.updated="0">Overview</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:1</designator><name date.updated="0">Generally</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">This chapter examines Manitoba&apos;s climate change strategy and the development of legislation in Manitoba, one of the mechanisms for the implementation of that strategy.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba was quick to embrace the challenge of matching the percentage greenhouse gas (&ldquo;GHG&rdquo;) reduction targets <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;3" nep=";;5" hyphenated="y" folio="8-3" backFolio="8-4" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>established for Canada under the Kyoto Protocol.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The Kyoto Protocol is an international agreement connected with the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change which sets binding targets for reducing 1990 GHG emission levels by an average of 5&percnt; between the years 2008 and 2012. Canada set a 6&percnt; reduction target. For further information see, United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, Kyoto Protocol, UN Doc FCCC/CP/1997/7Add.1, Dec. 10, 1997; 37 ILM 22 (1998), available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0ba8b6405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/6UUP-9CNG</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> It received early recognition for its leadership in this regard.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2005, Bloomberg Business Week magazine ranked Manitoba as the Number One regional government in the world in terms of battling climate change. It named Manitoba&apos;s then Premier Gary Doer among the Top 20 international leaders in the battle against climate change. See &ldquo;Battling Climate Change&rdquo;, Bloomberg Business Week, Online Extra (December 12, 2005). In 2008, The David Suzuki Foundation ranked Manitoba climate change policies in the top three provincial/territorial policies. See David Suzuki Foundation, &ldquo;Provincial Power Play&mdash;Breaking Away from Federal Inaction on Climate Change&rdquo; (2008), at p. 9, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0ba952805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/62P5-BSRX</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> <ital>The Climate Change and Emissions Reductions Act</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate Change and Emissions Reduction Act</ital>, C.C.S.M. c. C135 (repealed November 8, 2018).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> (&ldquo;<ital>The 2008 Climate Change Act</ital>&rdquo;) came into force in 2008 and solidified the province&apos;s commitment to achieving Canadian targets to reduce GHG emissions in line with Kyoto targets (6&percnt; below 1990 levels by 2012). The Act was later repealed by <ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>&rdquo;) which came into force on November 8, 2018.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba&apos;s target of 17.5 megatonnes (&ldquo;Mt&rdquo;) of GHGs by 2012 appeared achievable with reasonable effort and without major disruptions, economic or otherwise as of 2009. It became clear, however, that the much-touted objective would not be met when, in 2010, the Manitoba Department of Conservation forecasted a gap of 2.7 Mt in meeting the 2012 target and conceded by the end of 2011 that the target would not be achieved, in part due to a failure to achieve the anticipated credits from exports of hydroelectricity.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Report to the Legislative Assembly&mdash;Performance Audits (December 2010), at p. 10, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0ba9eec05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/report-to-the-legislative-assembly-performance-audits/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba did successfully achieve its interim goal of stabilizing GHG emissions at 2000 levels by 2010.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Environment Canada, Sixth National Report on Climate Change 2014 (2013), at p. 69, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0ba9eec15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">http://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2014/ec/En4-73-2013-eng.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2017, Manitoba garnered a negative report from Manitoba&apos;s Office of the Auditor General which found that despite past efforts, &ldquo;there had been little change in Manitoba greenhouse gas emissions&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.11, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0baa15d05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Auditor General described <ital>inter alia</ital> the existence of &ldquo;several gaps in the planning, monitoring, and reporting processes for initiatives aimed at reducing greenhouse gas emissions and at adapting to climate change impacts&rdquo;. However, the Manitoba government was already in the process of developing a new &ldquo;Made-in-Manitoba&rdquo; plan that would &ldquo;pave the way&rdquo; for the province to be &ldquo;Canada&apos;s cleanest, greenest and most climate resilient province&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See Manitoba, &ldquo;Province Responds to Auditor General&apos;s Report on Managing Climate Change&rdquo; (October 25, 2017), available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0baa8b005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;42372</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Manitoba government then introduced <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>. The 2018 Act requires the Minister charged with administering the Act to develop a &ldquo;climate and green plan&rdquo;, establish GHG emissions reductions goals, and prepare an annual report. The Act further establishes: (i) an &ldquo;Expert Advisory Council&rdquo; (to provide ongoing advice and recommendations to the Minister); (ii) a &ldquo;Low Carbon Government Office&rdquo; (to advance within government entities, GHG emissions reduction and sustainable operation strategies); and (iii) a &ldquo;Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Fund&rdquo; (to provide support for various projects, studies and activities).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. C134, ss. 2, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote><trace>More recently, Manitoba introduced </trace><trace.deleted/><trace><ital>Manitoba &apos;s Plan to Net Zero</ital> , which sets an ambitious goal for the province by 2050.  As a comprehensive plan ,  it includes province-wide priorities, government action to lead the change, collaboration with Indigenous peoples, building the next generation of workers, and actions to take across all sectors.  In addition to reducing and eliminating emissions, Manitoba  aims at  removing or offsetting emissions through a combination of activities that could include land and water-based carbon sequestration, carbon capture utilization and storage (CCUS), carbon stored building materials, and carbon offsets.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025), online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba&apos;s provincial government has long recognized hydroelectric power as one of several characteristics that gives the province an advantage in its efforts to reduce GHG emissions. Virtually all electricity in the province is generated by hydroelectric stations operated by Manitoba Hydro, the province&apos;s Crown-owned monopoly electricity supplier. Op<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;5" nep=";;7" hyphenated="n" folio="8-5" backFolio="8-6" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>portunities also continue to exist for the development of wind power and more extensive use of geothermal energy.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The vastness of Manitoba is also viewed by its provincial government as an asset, providing opportunities for agriculture and forestry carbon sinks. Capitalizing on these natural carbon stores, Manitoba&apos;s Peatlands Stewardship Strategy includes the 2014 <ital>Peatlands Stewardship Act</ital>, which aims to balance responsible economic development with conservation of peatland ecosystems.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Peatlands Stewardship and Related Amendments Act</ital>, S.M. 2014, c. 27.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The legislation is the first of its kind in Canada and builds upon the existing ban against peat mining in provincial parks as well as in the site east of Lake Winnipeg where Pimachiowin Aki, the ancestral home of the Anishinaabeg, is located and which received UNESCO world heritage status in 2018.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Peatlands Stewardship and Related Amendments Act</ital>, S.M. 2014, c. 27. See also Manitoba, &ldquo;Province Introduces Woodland Caribou Recovery Strategy&rdquo;, News Release (April 30, 2014), available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0baa8b005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;30647</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For additional discussion of these and other initiatives, see heading <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0baab2105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:19">&sect;8:19</ref></cite>, Major Initiatives.</para.text></para></section.body></section></section.block></analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">II</designator><name date.updated="0">Manitoba&apos;s Climate Change Profile</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value></metadata></metadata.block><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">A</designator><name date.updated="0">GHG Emissions</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:2</designator><name date.updated="0">Historical Emissions</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2008, at the launch of Manitoba&apos;s five-year action plan on climate change, <ital>Beyond Kyoto: Manitoba&apos;s Green Future</ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0baad9205f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Science, Technology, Energy and Mines, Beyond Kyoto: Manitoba&apos;s Green Future&mdash;Next Steps: 2008 Action on Climate Change (2008)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> (&ldquo;2008 Action Plan&rdquo;), the province emitted approximately 21.9 Mt of GHGs, measured in tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent (&ldquo;CO<sub>2</sub>e&rdquo;). This represented an increase of nearly <trace.deleted/><trace>15&percnt;</trace> or <trace.deleted/><trace>3.1</trace> Mt, over Manitoba&apos;s 18.6 Mt of emissions in 1990<trace>.</trace><trace.deleted/><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace.deleted/><trace>Canadian Centre for Energy Information. &ldquo;Greenhouse Gas Emissions &ndash; National Inventory Report (NIR),&rdquo; (last modified 13 May 2025), online (website): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://energy-information.canada.ca/en/subjects/greenhouse-gas-emissions-national-inventory-report-nir</ref></cite></trace></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> It equated to a contribution of approximately 3&percnt; to Canada&apos;s national total of <trace.deleted/><trace>684</trace> Mt for 2<trace.deleted/><trace>024</trace>.<trace.deleted/><trace> In 2023, Manitoba&apos;s emissions per capita were 15.3 tonnes of C02e, which is 16 per cent below the Canadian average of 18.2 tonnes per capita.</trace></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2009, Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions were reduced by 1.37 Mt.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bab9c705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment Canada, Canada&apos;s Energy and GHG Emissions Projections: Reference Case 2006-2020-Provincial and Territorial Tables (March 2008)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Almost half of the 2009 reductions were attributed to four events: the scheduled permanent closure of a northern smelter; the changeover of a thermal generating plan from coal to natural gas; reduced emissions from a private natural gas pipeline compressor station; and closure of a paper mill due to economic considerations.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For discussion, see Manitoba Wildlands, &ldquo;Recession Lowers Manitoba GHG Emissions&rdquo;, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0babc3805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/9JQK-C2ER</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>From 2011 to 2018, Manitoba saw an increase in GHG emissions from 19.1 Mt to 22.2 Mt, with emissions slowly declining since 2018. Manitoba&apos;s historic growth of emissions has remained relatively flat and lower than the Canadian average. From 2005 to 2023, Manitoba&apos;s economy has grown more rapidly than emissions, with emissions intensity for the entire economy (GHG per GDP) declining by 25 per cent over this period. This progress reflects improvements in energy efficiency, the phase-out of coal, fuel switching, and other measures.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025) at 8, online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Despite past reductions, Manitoba&apos;s emissions increased on the whole from 1990-20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/>. Emissions for 20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/> totalled 21.<trace>4</trace><trace.deleted/> Mt of GHGs<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bac11a15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment Canada, National Inventory Report: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada, 1990-2012 (April 2014), Part 3, Annex 11, Table A11-14, p. 27</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bac38b05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2014/ec/En81-4-2012-3-eng.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> and continued to account for nearly 3&percnt; of Canada&apos;s national total emissions.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bac38b15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, Manitoba&apos;s Report on Climate Change for 2012, Department of Conservation and Water Stewardship (February 2014) at 6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bac5fc05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/D7M6-79S9</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Between 1990 and 20<trace>24</trace><trace.deleted/>, Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions grew by 1<trace>7</trace><trace.deleted/><trace>.</trace>&percnt;<trace.deleted/><?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;7" nep=";;9" hyphenated="n" folio="8-7" backFolio="8-8" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><trace>Moreover, 4 &percnt; of greenhouse gases from fossil fuels are also released through fugitive sources like production, processing or storage of oil and natural gas (includes venting and flaring). 35&percnt; of remaining emissions come from non-energy intensive sources like methane, 27&percnt; come from livestock, manure management and inorganic fertilizer use in the agriculture sector, 6&percnt; from methane from landfills and wastewater treatment in the waste sector and 2&percnt; from other industrial processes that do not use fossil fuels like production of ammonia, lime, and glass.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s Path to Net Zero&rdquo; (October 2025) at 6, online (pdf): <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/netzero/mb-path-to-net-zero.pdf</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Unlike neighbouring Saskatchewan which has seen a boom in oil and natural gas production and a corresponding jump in GHG emission rates, the Manitoba economy does not have an industrial sector that is predominant in the production of GHG emissions at the national level.</para.text></para